      <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>
AIR SEALING

Overall, your house is moderately leaky from an air leakage perspective, and I have identified a few locations that can be addressed:

** The air coming in underneath the kitchen island was the most obvious spot. It can be sealed up from the inside, near the kick plate.

** Your belly bag has a few holes in it that are allowing cold air in. These can be patched up with sheathing tape.

** There was some cold air detected coming through the furnace vents. This is also likely related to rips and tears that I observed in your belly bag. Overall, your belly bag was in quite good shape, but ensure every penetration through it is sealed up.

** Around the outside edge of both doors. The doors are older, and in reasonable condition, but you could replace weather stripping as needed, and ensure the latches are working as intended.

Savings: If you address the air leakage locations identified above, I estimate a modest 10% reduction in air leakage heat loss, but it could result in a larger reduction. This is estimated to save you $40 per year and decrease your annual energy usage by 1.2 GJ. Although the savings aren't huge, the increase in comfort will be noticeable, for relatively low effort/ expense.

There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program for performing air sealing, however you will enjoy the annual energy savings every year.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant between $715 and $1300 is available for improving the airtightness of your home based on the level achieved. This is one of the most cost-effective energy-saving measures you can undertake.

An air-sealing grant is available if the airtightness of your home is improved to achieve or exceed the air change rate target proposed above. Achieving any of the targets defined for your home typically requires that the work be performed by an air-sealing professional.
</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>
MAIN WALLS

The main exterior walls of your home, were estimated to be of 2x6 construction, with fibreglass batt insulation (R20) in the cavities. For your interests sake, I have modelled adding 3 additional inches (R15) of rigid extruded polystyrene insulation to the exterior surface of the entire wall. This R-value of insulation would bring your homes insulation value up to current building practice values. Due to the large amount of labour involved in re-siding a home, and the high cost, this recommendation should only be taken if you were interested in completing a siding upgrade.

Savings: By adding 3 inches (R15) of rigid foam extruded polystyrene insulation to all exterior walls, you could potentially save $ 560 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 17 GJ, and qualify for up to a $2,500 rebate from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program ÂinsulationÂ rebate, a pre-approval is required and my EnerGuide evaluation of your home satisfies the pre-approval requirement. The rebate application, copies of sales receipts, and photos that clearly show where the insulation was added (and the thickness) must be received within three months of completing your insulation upgrade. For a full rebate, insulation products must be purchased in the Northwest Territories. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of up to $4,940 is available for adding insulation to exterior wall area of your home. Insulation grants are based on the percentage of wall area to which the insulation has been added and the amount of insulation added. A minimum of 20% of the exterior wall area of your home, excluding foundation walls, must be insulated in order to qualify for a grant.

</mainWalls>
                <doors>
DOORS

The two exterior doors in your home are older, but considered to be in reasonable condition. The savings on new doors will be minimal. Weather stripping was noted to be in need of minor repairs at both doors. There are currently no rebates available from the GNWT for door replacements.

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $162.50 per hinged door system is available when an eligible ENERGY STAR certified door or door system is installed. Replacing old, damaged, or leaky hinged doors with new ENERGY STAR certified products can help you save on energy and improve comfort. To determine eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that it is included on the list of eligible equipment as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant webpage.
</doors>
                <HotWaterSystem>
Propane Instantaneous Upgrade from Electric

You currently have a 182 litre conventional electric hot water tank. Although electric hot water tanks are efficient from the perspective that 100% of the electricity used either heats the water inside or the heat radiating from the tank supplements the heating of the home, electricity is very costly. During the evaluation of your house, we discussed your water tank based on its location, and the difficulty that may pose for on-demand hot water. I modelled replacing your electric hot water heater with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.95 or higher, just for your interests sake.

Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially save approximately $1010 per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.
</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>
FURNACE UPGRADE

Your current oil furnace is an older, 80% efficient, furnace with direct vent, sealed combustion, meaning there is no interaction with air in the home. During our discussion, you mentioned being uninterested in the propane furnaces, which is fine, but I've modelled upgrading to an ENERGY STARÂ® qualified, high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 97% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. I modelled this just to show you what your savings would be if you switched to propane. 

PROPANE FURNACE

Savings:

By replacing your current oil furnace with a 97% AFUE condensing propane furnace that incorporates an ECM fan blower you could potentially save $1060 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 22 GJ, and qualify for up to $1,500 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors.

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:
 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT:

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant initiative, a grant of $1,600 is available for replacing an oil-fired furnace with a 97% AFUE ENERGY STAR certified propane fired furnace. To determine the eligibility of the product you are seeking to purchase, confirm that the unit is included on the Searchable product list of eligible furnaces or boilers for the Canada Greener Homes Grant
</HeatingSystem>
                <AdditionalComments>
ADDITIONAL COMMENTS

CANADA GREENER HOMES GRANT

Through the Canada Greener Homes Grant, homeowners are eligible for up to $600 for the cost of pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and up to $5,000 total for the implementation of eligible retrofits completed after December 1, 2020.

Hiring a contractor is the recommended option in undertaking several of the home retrofits and for some retrofits it is a requirement. The contractor is responsible for complying with local bylaws and relevant provincial, territorial and federal legislation and guidelines.

ELIGIBILITY INFORMATION

For full details on eligibility requirements, eligible measures and grants for the recommended measures outlined below, please consult the Canada Greener Homes Grant website at canada.ca/greener-homes-grant or your Homeowner Information Kit.

Many eligible retrofit measures under the Canada Greener Homes Grant have certain conditions for eligibility. These include:

Â In order to be eligible for reimbursement for your retrofit, you must complete at least one retrofit that is both eligible and recommended by an energy advisor in this Renovation Upgrade Report
Â If you wish to undertake resiliency measures or purchase a thermostat, you must also undertake another eligible energy efficiency measure in order to qualify for a grant.
Â Purchased equipment such as heat pumps and windows must meet eligibility criteria and must be on the eligible product list as found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website. This information can also be found in the Homeowner Information Kit
Â All mechanical and electrical systems, with the exception of thermostats, must be installed by a licensed and trained professional
Â It is highly recommended that you use the attestation forms found on the Canada Greener Homes Grant website and Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal (if applicable for your retrofit) confirming that:
     o installation of an electrical or mechanical system has been completed by a trained and licensed professional*
     o an earth-energy (ground or water) system was installed in accordance with CSA standards
     o the heat pump has been sized to your entire home
*When using a licensed professional it is highly recommended that you obtain proof of their licence to install equipment in your province or territory.
Â All products must be purchased in Canada. Online purchases are only eligible if they are ordered from an online distributor located in Canada.

ADDITIONAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR INCENTIVES

You may access and combine grant funding from the Canada Greener Homes Grant with funding from other retrofit grant programs in your region. The sum of the funding you receive from all sources must not exceed 100% of the total cost of the pre- and post-retrofit EnerGuide evaluations and of each eligible retrofit measure.

Participants will be required to declare through the Canada Greener Homes Grant Portal funding or rebates received from other sources.Programs in your region that you may be eligible for include the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program, administered by the Arctic Energy Alliance. The rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amounts and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

LED Lights

Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.

Rebates: In Yellowknife, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 

To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 

For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 
http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program

NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Depressurization Test = Pass

The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (kitchen range hood, bathroom fans and the clothes dryer), indicated a pressure drop of 1.9 Pascals. As this number is lower than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment should not cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. 

During my assessment of your home, I did see both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors present. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed and operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ventilation

Your home currently has the following mechanical ventilation that vents to the outside of the house:
       
        - two bathroom fans
        - a range hood
        - a clothes dryer
      
You may want to consider installing ENERGY STAR rated bathroom fans that seal well, use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odors without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Fuel Costs Â Yellowknife

The fuel values below may be different than current prices, as they have been averaged over the past 5 years to account for fuel price fluctuations over time. These prices have been used to calculate energy costs and energy savings in Yellowknife, for your report:

Electricity - $0.32 per kWh
Oil - $1.24 per liter 
Propane - $0.75 per liter delivered
Wood - $410 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested
Pellets - $5.79 per bag, $351.29 per tonne bulk delivered


-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Disclaimer

The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building. This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your Energy Advisor

Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.

Sincerely,

Scott Dowler, Energy Management Specialist
Arctic Energy Alliance
Phone: (867) 920-3333
Fax: (867) 873-0303
E-mail: scott.dowler@aea.nt.ca

Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high transportation costs, and climate change!
</AdditionalComments>
